    <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-4">
      How to Cut Wire Mesh Import Costs Without Sacrificing Quality
    </h1>

    <p class="text-theme-secondary text-lg mb-8">
      Last updated: September 2026 | 12 min read
    </p>

    <!-- Answer First -->
    <div class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg p-6 mb-8">
      <p class="text-theme-primary text-lg leading-relaxed">
        <strong>Quick answer:</strong> The six levers that cut wire mesh import costs without degrading product: (1) <strong>specify standard sizes</strong> instead of custom—custom widths and diameters add 5–20% and extend lead times; (2) <strong>compare quotes per kilogram</strong>, not per roll or per square meter, to expose hidden down-gauging; (3) <strong>fill your containers to 90%+ utilization</strong>—an empty quarter of a 20ft box is freight paid on air; (4) buy <strong>FOB, not CIF or DDP</strong>, once you have a forwarder—factory freight markups run $500–1,500 per box; (5) negotiate <strong>payment terms</strong> (30/70 T/T standard; 20/80 or L/C at sight on repeat volume); (6) <strong>consolidate SKUs</strong> into fewer, larger orders to cross factory price-break thresholds. Together these typically save 15–30% of total landed cost.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- TOC -->
    <div class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg p-6 mb-8">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-4">Table of Contents</h2>
      <ul class="space-y-2 text-theme-secondary">
        <li><a href="#spec-optimization" class="hover:text-theme-primary">1. Start With Specification Optimization</a></li>
