    <!-- Intro -->
    <section class="mb-12">
      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4 leading-relaxed">
        Wire mesh is a commodity product in a competitive market, which means the spread between a well-run import program and a careless one is wide—15–30% of landed cost is typical when you add up spec creep, underfilled containers, freight markups, and poor payment terms. None of these savings require accepting thinner wire or lower-grade steel. They come from how you buy, not what you settle for.
      </p>
      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4 leading-relaxed">
        This guide assumes you already source from Anping or are comparing factory-direct quotes. If you are earlier in the process, start with our <a href="/procurement-process/" class="text-theme-accent hover:underline">full procurement process walkthrough</a>, then return here to optimize each stage. For the full import mechanics, see <a href="/blog/how-to-import-wire-mesh-from-china/" class="text-theme-accent hover:underline">how to import wire mesh from China</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- 1. Spec optimization -->
    <section id="spec-optimization" class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-4">1. Start With Specification Optimization</h2>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4 leading-relaxed">
        Every deviation from a factory's standard configuration costs money. Anping weaving and welding lines are set up for standard wire diameters (0.5, 0.6, 0.8, 1.0, 1.2, 1.5, 2.0, 2.5, 3.0 mm), standard roll widths (1.0, 1.2, 1.5, 2.0, 2.5 m), and standard roll lengths (10, 25, 30 m). Ask for 1.35 mm wire or 1.37 m width and the factory must either re-tool or cut down from the next standard—either way, you pay.
      </p>

      <ul class="list-disc list-inside text-theme-secondary space-y-2 mb-6">
        <li><strong>Round wire diameter to the nearest standard size up.</strong> Going from a custom 0.9 mm to standard 1.0 mm adds 23% more steel but often <em>lowers</em> the total quote, because the factory runs it as a standard product. Your mesh is also stronger.</li>
        <li><strong>Accept standard roll lengths.</strong> A 27 m roll means a partial setup, edge trim waste, and a special packing list. Standard 30 m rolls load better and quote lower per meter.</li>
        <li><strong>Right-size, not over-spec.</strong> If 304 stainless satisfies the corrosion requirement, do not write 316L out of habit—the alloy difference alone can be 30–50% of material cost. Match the spec tables in our <a href="/spec-tables/" class="text-theme-accent hover:underline">spec tables library</a> to your actual service conditions, and compare our <a href="/products/stainless-steel-wire-mesh/" class="text-theme-accent hover:underline">stainless steel wire mesh</a> options by grade before committing.</li>
        <li><strong>Delete cosmetic requirements.</strong> Bright annealed finish, paper interleaving between every layer, and custom-printed labels each add cost with zero functional value for most industrial uses. Keep them only where the end customer pays for them.</li>
      </ul>

      <div class="bg-theme-secondary bg-opacity-10 border border-theme rounded-lg p-6">
        <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-theme-primary mb-2">Rule of Thumb</h3>
        <p class="text-theme-secondary text-sm">
          Before sending an RFQ, ask the factory for their <strong>standard product list</strong> and spec as close to it as functionally possible. A spec that matches the factory's standard line is 5–20% cheaper than an equivalent custom spec at the same nominal performance.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 2. Weight-normalized -->
    <section id="weight-normalized" class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-4">2. Compare Quotes on Weight-Normalized Price</h2>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4 leading-relaxed">
        Factories quote mesh by the roll, by the square meter, by the panel, or by the kilogram—and each unit hides a different trick. A roll quoted at $22 can be cheaper or dearer than one quoted at $25 depending on wire diameter and roll length. The only unit that cannot be gamed is <strong>price per kilogram of mesh</strong>, because weight is directly proportional to the steel you actually receive.
      </p>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4">
        Normalize every quote before comparing:
      </p>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto mb-6">
        <table class="w-full text-sm border-collapse">
          <thead>
            <tr class="bg-theme-card border-b border-theme">
              <th class="text-left p-3 text-theme-primary font-semibold">Step</th>
              <th class="text-left p-3 text-theme-primary font-semibold">What to Do</th>
              <th class="text-left p-3 text-theme-primary font-semibold">Why It Matters</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="text-theme-secondary">
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">1. Calculate theoretical weight</td>
              <td class="p-3">Use the mesh weight formula (wire diameter, mesh count, dimensions) or our <a href="/tools/welded-wire-mesh-weight-calculator/" class="text-theme-accent hover:underline">weight calculator</a></td>
              <td class="p-3">Gives you the expected kg per roll to compare against the quoted weight</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">2. Convert quote to $/kg</td>
              <td class="p-3">Roll price ÷ quoted roll weight</td>
              <td class="p-3">Exposes quotes that look cheap per roll but contain thin wire</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">3. Compare against metal cost</td>
              <td class="p-3">Check current wire rod index (low-carbon steel ~$550–650/t; 304 stainless ~$2,300–2,700/t)</td>
              <td class="p-3">A $/kg quote below raw-material-plus-reasonable-processing signals substitution risk</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">4. Lock weight into the PO</td>
              <td class="p-3">State theoretical weight per roll with tolerance (typically −0% / +5%) and weighbridge verification</td>
              <td class="p-3">Makes underweight rolls a contract violation you can claim on</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4">
        Example: two factories quote "1 m × 30 m roll of 304 stainless woven mesh, 40 mesh, 0.25 mm wire." Factory A: $240. Factory B: $268. Theoretical weight is 24.5 kg. If A's roll actually weighs 21 kg (shaved wire) and B's weighs 24.5 kg, the normalized prices are $11.4/kg versus $10.9/kg—B is cheaper <em>and</em> honest. Never compare wire mesh quotes without the weight column.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- 3. Container utilization -->
    <section id="container-utilization" class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-4">3. Maximize Container Utilization</h2>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4 leading-relaxed">
        Ocean freight is a fixed cost per container. A 20ft FCL from Tianjin to Rotterdam or Los Angeles runs $1,500–3,500 depending on the market—whether you ship 12 tons or 26 tons in it. Every cubic meter of air inside the box is freight you paid for and received nothing for. Dense mesh products (welded panels, gabions, heavy woven mesh) hit the 28-ton payload limit before they fill the box; light products (fine stainless mesh, chain link, hex netting) fill the box long before they weigh it out.
      </p>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4">
        The two failure modes and their fixes:
      </p>

      <ul class="list-disc list-inside text-theme-secondary space-y-2 mb-6">
        <li><strong>Volume-limited orders (light mesh):</strong> Fill to the container's cubic capacity. Rework roll diameters or lengths with the factory so rolls pack tighter—sometimes switching from 30 m to 25 m rolls lifts utilization from 80% to 97% because of how diameters stack.</li>
        <li><strong>Weight-limited orders (heavy mesh/panels):</strong> You will weigh out first, so make the weight work: add a pallet of higher-value small-diameter mesh or accessories (posts, clamps, gates) on top of the heavy base load instead of shipping them separately by LCL.</li>
        <li><strong>Never guess.</strong> Run every order through the <a href="/tools/container-loading-calculator/" class="text-theme-accent hover:underline">container loading calculator</a> before confirming quantities, and send the factory the resulting loading plan so their packing matches it.</li>
      </ul>

      <div class="bg-theme-secondary bg-opacity-10 border border-theme rounded-lg p-6">
        <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-theme-primary mb-2">Worked Example</h3>
        <p class="text-theme-secondary text-sm">
          A buyer imports 24,000 m² of PVC-coated chain link annually in four LCL shipments of 6,000 m², paying roughly $95/CBM all-in for ocean plus destination charges. By switching to one annual 20ft FCL (~28,000 m² capacity at $2,400 freight), freight cost per m² drops from ~$0.055 to ~$0.029—a 47% freight saving, plus only one customs entry fee instead of four. The tradeoff is inventory carrying cost; at typical warehousing rates the math still wins above ~15,000 m²/year.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 4. Incoterms -->
    <section id="incoterms" class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-4">4. Choose the Right Incoterm: FOB vs CIF vs DDP</h2>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4 leading-relaxed">
        The Incoterm decides who controls each leg of transport—and whoever controls a leg sets its price. Factories quoting CIF or DDP almost never book the cheapest freight; they book the freight that is easiest for them, or they add margin. Common markups we see on factory-arranged freight: $500–1,500 per 20ft container versus what a forwarder quotes for the same sailing.
      </p>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto mb-6">
        <table class="w-full text-sm border-collapse">
          <thead>
            <tr class="bg-theme-card border-b border-theme">
              <th class="text-left p-3 text-theme-primary font-semibold">Incoterm</th>
              <th class="text-left p-3 text-theme-primary font-semibold">Typical Cost Impact</th>
              <th class="text-left p-3 text-theme-primary font-semibold">When to Use It</th>
              <th class="text-left p-3 text-theme-primary font-semibold">Watch Out For</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="text-theme-secondary">
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">FOB Xingang (Tianjin)</td>
              <td class="p-3">Baseline—your forwarder's rate, no markup</td>
              <td class="p-3">Default for repeat importers with any forwarder</td>
              <td class="p-3">You handle insurance and destination charges</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">EXW Anping</td>
              <td class="p-3">FOB minus $30–80/box, plus your China-side costs</td>
              <td class="p-3">Buyers with a consolidation warehouse or agent in China</td>
              <td class="p-3">Inland trucking + export customs run $300–600 per container if you have no China agent</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">CFR / CIF your port</td>
              <td class="p-3">+$500–1,500 freight markup typical</td>
              <td class="p-3">First small order with no forwarder; samples</td>
              <td class="p-3">No control over sailing schedule or carrier; CIF insurance is minimum cover</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">DDP delivered</td>
              <td class="p-3">+8–15% on goods value for freight, duty handling, and margin</td>
              <td class="p-3">Only for urgent small parcels and sample orders</td>
              <td class="p-3">Factory may underdeclare value to you—your compliance risk at audit</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4">
        The practical move: get the factory's CIF quote, then strip the freight out using the <a href="/tools/fob-price-calculator/" class="text-theme-accent hover:underline">FOB price calculator</a>, and compare against your forwarder's all-in FOB-to-door rate. If the factory's freight component exceeds your forwarder's quote by more than $200 per box, book it yourself. Keep CIF only for your very first order while you have no forwarder relationship.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- 5. Payment terms -->
    <section id="payment-terms" class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-4">5. Negotiate Payment-Term Savings</h2>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4 leading-relaxed">
        Payment terms are a financing cost, and like freight, they are negotiable. The standard opening structure—30% T/T deposit, 70% against B/L copy—ties up your cash for the production period plus transit. On a $60,000 order at an 8% annual cost of capital, 60 days of tied-up cash costs about $790. Shifting terms recovers real money:
      </p>

      <ul class="list-disc list-inside text-theme-secondary space-y-2 mb-6">
        <li><strong>First order:</strong> 30/70 T/T is the floor. Do not accept 50% deposits from a new supplier—anything above 30% upfront increases your exposure without improving their security meaningfully.</li>
        <li><strong>Second order, same factory:</strong> Ask for 20/80. Most Anping factories accept this once the first shipment cleared without claims.</li>
        <li><strong>Volume commitments:</strong> A 12-month rolling forecast with quarterly releases can buy you 30-day open account terms after 3–4 clean cycles. This is the single biggest working-capital win available in this trade.</li>
        <li><strong>Pay the deposit promptly.</strong> Factories price hesitation into quotes; buyers who pay deposits within 48 hours of PO signature consistently get better pricing on repeat orders than buyers who sit on paperwork for two weeks.</li>
        <li><strong>Bank charges:</strong> Use shared charges (SHA) on T/T, and batch payments where possible. $25–50 per transfer is trivial once, but across 20 orders a year it is real money.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <!-- 6. Consolidate -->
    <section id="consolidate" class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-4">6. Consolidate SKUs and Order Volume</h2>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4 leading-relaxed">
        Price breaks in Anping are mostly weight-based. A factory's quote curve typically steps down at 1 ton, 5 tons, 10 tons, and 25 tons. Splitting your annual demand across six small orders from four suppliers means you pay the highest tier price six times, pay six sets of local charges, and spend six times the management effort. Consolidation is almost always the biggest single saving available to mid-size buyers.
      </p>

      <ul class="list-disc list-inside text-theme-secondary space-y-2 mb-6">
        <li><strong>One order, one container, one factory.</strong> Single-SKU containers maximize volume discounts and simplify QC. If you need five mesh types, price the all-in consolidated quote against the sum of best individual quotes—the consolidated quote usually wins once freight is added.</li>
        <li><strong>Standardize across projects.</strong> If two projects both need welded mesh, pick one 50×50 mm × 3 mm spec and adjust on-site rather than buying 50×50 and 60×60 as separate SKUs. Each unique SKU costs you a price break.</li>
        <li><strong>Group orders with sister companies or distributors.</strong> Buyers who cannot fill a container alone routinely consolidate with neighboring importers through a shared forwarder, splitting freight and customs-entry savings.</li>
        <li><strong>Watch the cash tradeoff.</strong> Consolidation raises per-order inventory. Run your carrying-cost number before doubling order size; the freight and price-break savings usually beat it above roughly 10 tons/year, but verify for your balance sheet.</li>
      </ul>

      <div class="bg-theme-secondary bg-opacity-10 border border-theme rounded-lg p-6">
        <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-theme-primary mb-2">Savings Stack Estimate</h3>
        <p class="text-theme-secondary text-sm">
          Typical ranges per lever on a 20ft-container order from Anping: spec optimization 3–8%, weight-normalized supplier selection 3–10%, container utilization 5–15% of freight, Incoterm switch 2–6%, payment terms 0.5–1.5%, SKU consolidation 4–12%. They compound multiplicatively, not additively—combined, 15–30% of landed cost is realistic and repeatable.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- FAQ -->
    <section id="faq" class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-6">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

      <div class="space-y-4">
        <details class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg">
          <summary class="p-4 font-semibold text-theme-primary cursor-pointer">Will optimizing specs really save money if the mesh gets heavier?</summary>
          <div class="p-4 pt-0 text-theme-secondary">
            Yes, because standard sizes eliminate setup, retooling, and trim waste at the factory. A custom 0.9 mm wire requires a drawing die change and small-lot handling; the standard 1.0 mm wire runs on the main line. Buyers routinely see the heavier standard product quote lower than the lighter custom one—plus they receive more steel. The savings come from production efficiency, not from accepting less.
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg">
          <summary class="p-4 font-semibold text-theme-primary cursor-pointer">How much can a container loading calculator actually save?</summary>
          <div class="p-4 pt-0 text-theme-secondary">
            On volume-limited cargo (light mesh), the difference between 75% and 95% utilization on a $2,500 ocean leg is about $0.006 per usable m²-equivalent across the container—roughly 20–26% of the freight cost per unit. On a 20,000 m² annual import program that is $1,200–2,000 a year from one calculation. The <a href="/tools/container-loading-calculator/" class="text-theme-accent hover:underline">container loading calculator</a> pays for itself on the first order.
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg">
          <summary class="p-4 font-semibold text-theme-primary cursor-pointer">Is CIF ever the right choice for wire mesh?</summary>
          <div class="p-4 pt-0 text-theme-secondary">
            For a first order of one or two pallets with no forwarder relationship, yes—it gets goods moving without you booking anything. Beyond that, switch to FOB. If you stay on CIF for convenience, at least strip the freight component with the <a href="/tools/fob-price-calculator/" class="text-theme-accent hover:underline">FOB price calculator</a> and check it against independent forwarder quotes, so you know the markup you are paying.
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg">
          <summary class="p-4 font-semibold text-theme-primary cursor-pointer">What is a fair wire mesh price per kilogram?</summary>
          <div class="p-4 pt-0 text-theme-secondary">
            It tracks raw material: low-carbon galvanized mesh typically lands at $0.80–1.30/kg FOB, stainless 304 woven mesh at $5–9/kg depending on fineness, and heavy welded panels at $0.90–1.40/kg. Quotes far below these bands almost always mean thinner wire, lower zinc, or grade substitution. Use weight-normalized comparison and the material index check described above before celebrating a cheap quote.
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg">
          <summary class="p-4 font-semibold text-theme-primary cursor-pointer">How do I know when an order is big enough to consolidate?</summary>
          <div class="p-4 pt-0 text-theme-secondary">
            Two tests: (1) total annual volume across all SKUs exceeds about 10 tons or 15 CBM—then consolidation almost always beats splitting; (2) your top two SKUs alone can fill 70%+ of a container. Below those thresholds, LCL or shared consolidation through a forwarder is usually cheaper than forcing full containers. Model both with the loading calculator before deciding.
          </div>
        </details>
      </div>
    </section>

